WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.159
As many of us head home for the day

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.340
some animals are just getting their day started.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.769
Sometimes mistaken for hummingbirds,
hungry hawkmths hover in mid-air

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:14.569
sipping nectar from flowers, pollinating
them in return.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.140
These agile insects maneuver
their way around flowers

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.820
that sway in the wind.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.240
Often, with little light to see by.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.680
To test how one species of hawkmoth

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:29.269
called the tobacco hornworm keeps up
with its moving flower targets,

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:32.229
researchers created robotic flowers.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.020
There's been a lot of curiosity about

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.940
how animals are able to

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.020
adjust their visual systems in order to
be able to see

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.340
at low light levels.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.440
The hawkmoths didn't seem to mind that the flowers were fake.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.600
As long as they got their sweet sugary
snack.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.260
The researchers tested how well the moths track

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.260
the movement of the flowers under two

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.780
lighting conditions: early dusk and much

00:00:54.789 --> 00:00:58.809
darker moonlight. When the flowers swayed
back and forth

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:02.800
no more than twice per second, the moths
kept up just fine --

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.010
regardless of lighting. Any faster

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.610
and they started making mistakes
especially in the dimmest light

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.770
which also caused them to lag well
behind the flowers.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.880
The researchers think the moths' visual
system

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.700
slows down in dim light to boost their 
visual acuity,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.180
much like the shutter of a camera.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.180
That works fine as long as an object is

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.020
moving slowly

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.900
but speed things up images start to blur.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.340
So cameras and moth eyes can't keep up.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.840
But this may not actually matter in the wild.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.900
The researchers also found that the kinds of
flowers these hawkmoths prefer

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.360
naturally sway in the wind, no faster

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.740
than the insect's visual system can handle.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.820
It's possible plant and pollinator

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.480
have co-evolved to be
well matched for each other

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.460
as the light fades.

